    "q-213339": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213339",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $9,$ the additive inverse of $15$ modulo $9$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $15$ modulo $9$ is the integer $0\\le x<9$ such that\n\n$$\nx+15≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $15≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & ≡1\\cdot 9+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-6+6≡0,$ so $x≡-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡9-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $15$ modulo $9$ is $3.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15+3≡18≡2\\cdot 9≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
