    "q-213340": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213340",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $16,$ the additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $28$ modulo $16$ is the integer $0\\le x<16$ such that\n\n$$\nx+28≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $28≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡1\\cdot 16+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-12+12≡0,$ so $x≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡0-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡16-12 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16) \\\\  & ≡4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $28$ modulo $16$ is $4.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28+4≡32≡2\\cdot 16≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
