    "q-213239": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213239",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $13,$ the additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $-50$ modulo $13$ is the integer $0\\le x<13$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-50)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-50≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & ≡(-4)\\cdot 13+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡(-4)\\cdot 0+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-2+2≡0,$ so $x≡-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡0-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡13-2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\  & ≡11 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-50$ modulo $13$ is $11.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50+11≡-39≡(-3)\\cdot 13≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
